abstract: >
  Pre-registered measurements of the cost and friction of converting
  accumulated L2 USDC micropayment revenue into spendable fiat. The
  usdc-fiat-sweep-api experiment (v1.0.1) converted 8 tranches totaling
  $157.25908 USDC to USD via the Coinbase Advanced Trade convert API:
  the convert fee quoted and charged was $0.00, the realized fee was 0.00%,
  and the USD spread was 0% on every tranche from $1 to the full wallet
  balance; the only realized cost was Base gas, 0.00000654 ETH spent from a
  0.00014 ETH (approximately $0.31) funding float. The usdc-fiat-sweep-v2
  experiment (v2.0.0) replicated the endpoints on an independent exchange
  (Kraken USDC/USD) at x402 micro-receipt volumes ($5-$20): the realized fee
  was exactly the advertised 0.20% taker fee on both executed USD sell
  tranches, with no hidden spread, and the Coinbase issuer rail reproduced
  the 1:1 $0.00-fee continuity finding; the CAD disbursement arm is recorded
  as not_executed_below_minimum due to a sealed residue-formula design
  conflict, itself the most reproducible result of the pass. Figure 3 (fee
  decomposition) is intentionally absent because every cost component is
  zero.
keywords:
  - pre-registered-experiment
  - reproducibility
  - payments
  - treasury
